{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Los Angeles Dodgers announced themselves immediately, with catcher Will Smith's two-run blast on Opening Day setting the tone for a team that would finish with 100 wins. March and April were mostly business as usual for this star-studded roster — Kyle Tucker, the major offseason acquisition, announced himself with a go-ahead single in a dramatic April 27th comeback, and Max Muncy provided pop throughout an early stretch that had the Dodgers sitting comfortably above .500 as summer approached.\n\nThe summer months brought the Dodgers to their apex and then briefly tested their resolve. June was dominant at 18-9, headlined by Dalton Rushing's walk-off single and Teoscar Hernández's Independence Day grand slam. But August proved turbulent — Edwin Díaz surrendered a crushing home run on August 7th, then served up back-to-back extra-base hits to Arizona the following night, and the bullpen showed cracks as the Dodgers stumbled to a 13-14 record in the dog days. Yet even in a losing month, Max Muncy provided a three-run blast on August 13th, and Tommy Edman chipped in clutch singles to remind the league this roster never truly goes quietly.\n\nSeptember was the Dodgers' best month of the year. Hernández's two-run double on September 3rd ignited a stretch that saw Los Angeles go 18-7 down the stretch, with Mookie Betts blasting a three-run homer and Tommy Edman adding a clutch three-run shot to put the division away in style. The team finished 100-62, scoring 801 runs and posting a 3.55 ERA — a complete team in every sense.\n\nNow the Dodgers are alive and dangerous in the postseason. They've already dispatched their NLDS opponents, with Teoscar Hernández homering in Game 1, Kyle Tucker adding his own shot, Freddie Freeman going deep in Game 2, and Enrique Hernández hitting a crucial home run in Game 3. The series continues, and with Andy Pages delivering a two-run single to push runs across in Game 4, the Dodgers are making noise in October once again. The full story is still being written.",
